import json

# 30 new Wonders (12 Oct 2026), timeless and fact-checked, reusing existing emblem pics so no new art is needed.
# Format matches the existing file. One Bangla gloss per story in (বাংলা - meaning) form.
NEW = [
 {"id":"speed-of-light-limit","cat":"physics","pic":"orbit","title":"Nothing can go faster than light - and that makes time bend","story":"Light moves at about 300,000 kilometres a second, and this is not just fast - it is the universe's speed limit (গতিসীমা - a limit on speed). Nothing with mass can reach it. Stranger still: the closer something goes to light speed, the slower its own clock ticks compared to yours. This is not a trick of the clock; time itself stretches. Astronauts who travelled fast have returned a tiny fraction of a second younger than they would have been on Earth. The faster you move through space, the slower you move through time.",
  "also":["GPS satellites must correct for this time-bending, or your map would be wrong by kilometres within a day.","The limit is really the speed of cause and effect; light just happens to travel at it.","If you could ride a beam of light, no time would pass for you at all."],
  "think":"If moving fast slows your clock, is there one single 'now' for the whole universe?"},
 {"id":"atoms-mostly-empty","cat":"physics","pic":"atom","title":"You are almost entirely empty space","story":"An atom is a tiny nucleus (নিউক্লিয়াস - the dense centre) with electrons far, far away from it. If the nucleus were the size of a marble, the nearest electron would be hundreds of metres away, with nothing in between. So the solid-feeling chair you sit on is more than 99.9999% empty space. What stops your hand passing through the table is not stuff hitting stuff - it is the electric push between electrons. You never actually touch anything; you feel the force.","also":["The 'solidness' of matter comes from a quantum rule that says two electrons cannot share the same state.","All the empty space removed, the entire human race would fit in the volume of a sugar cube.","The same force that stops your hand is the force that lets you type on a keyboard."],
  "think":"If atoms are mostly empty, why does a wall feel so solid?"},
 {"id":"dna-information","cat":"biology","pic":"dna","title":"Your body runs on four-letter code",
  "story":"Every living thing is built from instructions written in DNA using just four chemical letters: A, T, G and C. Three letters make a 'word' that stands for a building block, and strings of these words are the recipes for everything your body makes. It is a code - real information, like the binary (বাইনারি - a code of just two symbols, 0 and 1) you learned the computer uses. A single cell holds about two metres of DNA, folded up, carrying more instructions than a very long book.",
  "also":["Your DNA is about 99.9% identical to every other human's; the 0.1% is all the difference.","You share roughly half your genes with a banana, because life reuses what works.","Scientists can now read and write this code, which is how some medicines are made."],
  "think":"If DNA is a code, what is the difference between it and the code you write in Python?"},
 {"id":"bacteria-outnumber","cat":"biology","pic":"ant","title":"You are carrying trillions of tiny helpers","story":"Your body has roughly as many bacterial cells as human cells - tens of trillions of them, mostly in your gut. Far from being dirty, most are essential: they help digest your food, make vitamins, and train your immune system (রোগ প্রতিরোধ ব্যবস্থা - the body's defence against disease). You are not a single creature but a walking community. When you eat, you are feeding them too, and what you feed them changes which ones thrive - and that can change how you feel.","also":["A baby gets its first bacteria from its mother during birth.","The bacteria in your gut weigh roughly as much as your brain.","Different people's gut communities are as unique as fingerprints."],
  "think":"If helpful bacteria live inside you, where is the line between 'you' and 'not you'?"},
 {"id":"deep-time","cat":"geography","pic":"mountain","title":"Mountains are rivers of rock, moving too slowly to see","story":"The ground under your feet is not still. Continents drift about as fast as your fingernails grow - a few centimetres a year - carried on vast plates (প্লেট - huge slabs of the Earth's shell) floating on hot rock below. Over millions of years this pushes up mountains, opens oceans, and closes them again. The Himalayas are still rising because India is still pushing into Asia. Everything that feels permanent - a mountain, a coastline - is just a slow-motion photograph of something moving.","also":["The Atlantic Ocean grows a few centimetres wider every year.","Mount Everest is made partly of rock that was once the bottom of a sea.","In 250 million years the continents may join into one supercontinent again."],
  "think":"If mountains move, what does it mean to say any place is 'where it has always been'?"},
 {"id":"ocean-currents","cat":"geography","pic":"wave","title":"A river of warm water keeps Europe from freezing","story":"The ocean has rivers inside it. One, the Gulf Stream, carries warm water from the tropics up past western Europe, keeping places like Britain far milder than their northern latitude (অক্ষাংশ - how far north or south a place is) should allow. These currents are driven by wind, by the spin of the Earth, and by water sinking as it cools and grows salty. They move more water than every river on land combined, and they share the planet's heat from the equator to the poles.","also":["Without the Gulf Stream, London's winters could resemble Canada's.","A rubber-duck spill in 1992 helped scientists map ocean currents as the ducks washed up for years.","These currents also carry heat that feeds storms."],
  "think":"If the ocean shares heat around the planet, what might happen if one current slowed down?"},
 {"id":"prime-numbers-guard","cat":"technology","pic":"lock","title":"Huge prime numbers guard your secrets","story":"A prime number can only be divided by 1 and itself: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11... Multiplying two large primes together is easy, but taking the answer apart to find the original primes is so hard that even the fastest computers would need longer than the age of the universe. This one-way difficulty is the lock on your messages, your bank, and your passwords (পাসওয়ার্ড). The security of almost the whole internet rests on a fact about numbers that mathematicians have studied for over two thousand years.","also":["There are infinitely many primes - the Greeks proved it long ago.","The largest known prime has more than 24 million digits.","If someone found a fast way to split big numbers into primes, much of today's encryption would break."],
  "think":"Why is it safe to build secrets on something being hard, rather than impossible?"},
 {"id":"packet-switching","cat":"technology","pic":"cable","title":"Your messages travel in pieces, by different roads","story":"When you send a photo, it does not travel as one piece. It is chopped into small packets (প্যাকেট - small bundles of data), each labelled with where it is going, and they are sent separately - often by different routes across the world - then reassembled at the other end. This is why the internet is so hard to break: if one road is blocked, the packets simply take another. The whole global network is built on this one clever idea, invented so that a message could always find a way through.","also":["A single web page can arrive as hundreds of packets from several different servers.","Packets can arrive out of order; your computer puts them back in sequence.","The same idea means a damaged network can heal around the damage."],
  "think":"If messages can always find another road, who could ever fully block the internet - and how?"},
 {"id":"photosynthesis","cat":"biology","pic":"leaf","title":"Plants eat light and breathe out the air you need","story":"A leaf is a solar machine. It takes sunlight, water, and the carbon dioxide you breathe out, and builds sugar - its food - releasing oxygen (অক্সিজেন) as the leftover. Almost every scrap of food you have ever eaten traces back to this, and almost every breath of oxygen you have ever taken came from a plant or tiny ocean algae doing it. The energy in your body right now is sunlight, caught by a leaf, passed up the food chain to you.","also":["Most of the world's oxygen comes not from forests but from tiny plankton in the sea.","A large tree can release enough oxygen in a year for two people.","The green colour is a pigment called chlorophyll, which catches the light."],
  "think":"If your energy came from sunlight caught by a plant, in what sense are you 'powered by the Sun'?"},
 {"id":"moon-tides","cat":"physics","pic":"moon","title":"The Moon lifts the whole ocean twice a day","story":"The Moon's gravity (মহাকর্ষ - the pull between any two masses) tugs on the Earth, and the oceans, being liquid, bulge toward it. As the Earth spins beneath this bulge, every coast rises and falls - the tides - usually twice a day. The Sun pulls too, and when Sun and Moon line up, the tides grow largest. The Moon is slowly stealing a little of Earth's spin through these tides, so our days are getting longer by a tiny fraction of a second each century.","also":["The Moon is drifting away from Earth by about 4 centimetres a year.","Long ago, when the Moon was closer, a day lasted only about six hours.","Even the solid ground rises and falls a little with the tides."],
  "think":"If the tides are slowing Earth's spin, what does that tell you about where that energy goes?"},
 {"id":"fire-is-sunlight","cat":"chemistry","pic":"flame","title":"A fire is sunlight escaping from wood","story":"Burning is the opposite of what a plant did to grow. The tree used sunlight to lock energy into wood; fire breaks those bonds and lets the energy out again as heat and light, combining the wood with oxygen (জারণ - combining with oxygen) from the air. The flame is glowing gas, so hot it shines. When you sit by a fire, you are warmed by sunlight that a tree caught years ago and stored - you are feeling an old summer being released.","also":["A candle flame is hottest and bluest at its base, where oxygen is richest.","Fire needs three things - fuel, heat and oxygen; remove any one and it stops.","In space, with no gravity to pull hot air up, a flame burns as a small blue ball."],
  "think":"If fire releases stored sunlight, what are coal and oil, which also burn?"},
 {"id":"evolution-finch","cat":"biology","pic":"finch","title":"Living things are shaped by who survives to have young","story":"In any group of animals, the young differ a little. If a difference helps one survive and raise young in its surroundings, that difference becomes more common in the next generation. Over very long times, this slow sorting - called natural selection (প্রাকৃতিক নির্বাচন) - reshapes a species to fit its world. On one group of islands, finches' beaks changed shape over generations to match the seeds they could find. Nobody designed the beak; the island's hard seeds did the choosing, one generation at a time.","also":["The same process gave whales their shape from land-walking ancestors.","Bacteria evolve fast, which is why some medicines stop working over time.","Evolution has no goal; it only ever fits a creature to right now."],
  "think":"If no one is choosing, how can a process with no plan produce something as fitting as a beak?"},
 {"id":"zero-invention","cat":"philosophy","pic":"zero","title":"Someone had to invent nothing","story":"It sounds obvious, but writing a symbol for 'nothing' was one of the hardest ideas humans ever had. For most of history, counting systems had no zero, which made arithmetic clumsy and a number like 105 hard to write without confusion. The idea of zero as a number you can calculate with grew in ancient India and travelled the world. Without it there would be no place value, no algebra as you know it, and no computers - because the whole machine is built on the difference between something and nothing, 1 and 0.","also":["The word 'zero' travelled from Sanskrit through Arabic into European languages.","Roman numerals had no zero, which is why they are so awkward to add.","Every computer is, at heart, a vast arrangement of zeros and ones."],
  "think":"If zero means 'nothing', why is it one of the most powerful ideas in all of mathematics?"},
 {"id":"logic-gates","cat":"technology","pic":"logic","title":"A computer is millions of tiny yes-or-no switches","story":"Underneath every app and game, a computer does only one kind of thing, billions of times a second: it answers true-or-false questions with tiny switches called logic gates (গেট - a switch that follows a rule). One gate says 'true only if both inputs are true'; another says 'true if either is'. Wire enough of them together and you can add numbers, store memory, and run everything. The whole dazzling machine is built from the simplest idea there is - on or off - repeated at an unimaginable scale.","also":["A modern chip can hold tens of billions of these switches.","The same and/or/not logic runs in your if-statements in Python.","The switches are so small that thousands would fit across a human hair."],
  "think":"How can something as rich as a video game be built from only 'on' and 'off'?"},
 {"id":"half-life","cat":"chemistry","pic":"tape","title":"Some atoms carry a clock that never speeds up or slows down","story":"Certain atoms are unstable and break apart at random - but a huge pile of them decays at a perfectly steady rate called a half-life (অর্ধায়ু - the time for half to decay): the time for half of them to go. Nothing changes it - not heat, not pressure. This makes radioactive atoms into natural clocks. By measuring how much of a certain atom is left in an old bone or rock, scientists can read its age - how we know some fossils are millions of years old, and how the age of the Earth was found.","also":["Carbon dating uses a form of carbon to date things up to about 50,000 years old.","The Earth's age, about 4.5 billion years, was found this way.","A single atom's moment of decay is one of nature's purely random events."],
  "think":"If each atom decays at random, how can a whole pile keep such perfect time?"},
 {"id":"compound-interest","cat":"society","pic":"coin","title":"Growth that feeds on itself becomes astonishing","story":"If something grows by the same fraction each step, and the growth is added back in, it does not grow in a straight line - it curves upward, faster and faster. This is compound growth (চক্রবৃদ্ধি - growth added back on itself). A small saving that grows 10% a year more than doubles in under eight years, and keeps accelerating. The same maths rules a spreading rumour, a virus, a population, and the interest on a debt. People are famously bad at feeling how fast it goes, which is why it surprises everyone, for good and for ill.","also":["Fold a paper 42 times and it would reach the Moon - doubling is that powerful.","The same curve describes both savings growing and debts spiralling.","A single grain of rice doubled on each square of a chessboard would bury a country."],
  "think":"Why are humans so bad at guessing where fast-compounding growth will end up?"},
 {"id":"placebo","cat":"biology","pic":"body","title":"Believing a cure works can make it work a little","story":"If you give someone a sugar pill but tell them it is medicine, a surprising number feel genuinely better - less pain, less worry. This is the placebo effect (প্লাসিবো - a fake treatment that still helps): the mind's expectation changing the body. It is real enough that every new medicine must beat a placebo in testing, or we cannot tell if the medicine itself did anything. The brain is not separate from the body; what you expect can shift your heartbeat, your pain, even your immune response.","also":["Placebos can work even when people are told they are placebos.","A bigger or more colourful pill often produces a stronger placebo effect.","This is why medical tests hide who got the real treatment from everyone involved."],
  "think":"If expecting to feel better can help, how would you ever prove a real medicine actually works?"},
 {"id":"timezones","cat":"geography","pic":"clock","title":"Noon is a different moment everywhere","story":"The Sun cannot be overhead everywhere at once, so 'noon' happens at different real moments around the world. To keep clocks sensible, humans sliced the planet into time zones (সময় অঞ্চল - regions that share one clock time). Cross a line, change your clock by an hour. It is a human invention, barely over a century old, created because trains and telegraphs suddenly let people coordinate across distances where the Sun no longer agreed with the timetable. When it is afternoon in Dhaka, it is the middle of the night in Canada.","also":["Before railways, each town kept its own time by the Sun.","A few places use half-hour or 45-minute offsets.","The date itself changes at an imaginary line in the Pacific Ocean."],
  "think":"If time zones are a human invention, is there any such thing as the 'real' time?"},
 {"id":"redundancy","cat":"technology","pic":"share","title":"The internet survives because nothing important has just one copy","story":"Important data is never kept in one place. It is copied across many machines in many buildings, so that if one fails - a fire, a flood, a broken cable - the others carry on and the lost copy is rebuilt from the rest. This is redundancy (অপ্রয়োজনীয় নকল - keeping spare copies on purpose), and it is why a video you watch is still there when a whole data centre goes dark. The same idea runs in nature: you have two kidneys, two lungs. Survival is built from spare copies.","also":["Your own backup habit - a second copy of your files - is redundancy at home.","Big services copy your data to several continents at once.","A system with no spare copy has a 'single point of failure' - the thing engineers fear most."],
  "think":"Where in your own life would a single point of failure hurt you, and what is the spare copy?"},
 {"id":"sound-speed","cat":"physics","pic":"wave","title":"You hear the past, and see a slightly older past","story":"Sound is slow. It travels about 340 metres a second through air - fast for a runner, crawling for a wave. That is why you see a distant firework burst before you hear it, and why thunder lags behind lightning: count the seconds between and you can measure how far the storm is. Light is almost a million times faster, so it arrives first. Every sound you hear is a tiny bit of the past, and the farther the source, the older the moment reaching your ears.","also":["Sound travels faster in water and faster still in steel than in air.","A thunderclap three seconds after the flash is about one kilometre away.","In the emptiness of space there is no air, so no sound travels at all."],
  "think":"If both light and sound carry the past, is anything you sense truly happening 'now'?"},
 {"id":"keystone-species","cat":"biology","pic":"leaf","title":"Remove one animal and a whole landscape can change","story":"Some species hold an entire ecosystem (বাস্তুতন্ত্র - a community of living things and their surroundings) together far beyond their numbers. When wolves were returned to one American park, they thinned the deer, so young trees grew back, so riverbanks held firm, so the rivers themselves changed course. One predator reshaped the land. These are keystone species - like the central stone of an arch; pull it out and the arch falls. It is a reminder that in a living system, everything is connected to everything, often in ways no one predicted.","also":["Sea otters protect kelp forests by eating the urchins that would devour them.","Elephants dig water holes and clear paths that dozens of other species use.","Losing a keystone species can collapse a web no one knew depended on it."],
  "think":"If one species can reshape a whole landscape, how carefully should we remove any part of a system?"},
 {"id":"map-projection","cat":"geography","pic":"globe","title":"Every flat map of the world is a lie - it has to be","story":"You cannot flatten a ball onto paper without stretching it somewhere. So every world map distorts (বিকৃত - changed from the true shape) something - size, shape, or distance. The most familiar map makes countries near the poles look huge: it shows Greenland as large as Africa, though Africa is really fourteen times bigger. There is no 'correct' flat map, only different honest compromises for different jobs. The map is never the territory; it is a chosen distortion, and knowing which one is chosen is how you read it wisely.","also":["Greenland looks bigger than Africa on common maps but is a fraction of its size.","Sailors' maps distort size to keep directions straight and true.","The only truly undistorted map of Earth is a globe."],
  "think":"If every flat map distorts something, how would you choose which distortion to trust for a given purpose?"},
 {"id":"catalyst","cat":"chemistry","pic":"atom","title":"Some things make change happen without being used up","story":"A catalyst (অনুঘটক - a helper that speeds a reaction) is a substance that makes a chemical change happen faster, yet comes out unchanged at the end, ready to help again. A tiny amount can transform mountains of material. Your body runs on them: enzymes are catalysts that let reactions which would otherwise take years happen in a blink, at body temperature. Factories use them to make fuels and medicines. It is a strange, powerful idea - a thing that causes enormous change while giving up nothing of itself.","also":["The enzymes in your spit begin breaking down food before you swallow.","A catalyst in your car's exhaust turns poison gases into safer ones.","Without enzymes, digesting a single meal could take far longer than a lifetime."],
  "think":"Where else - not just in chemistry - does a small, unchanged thing cause a huge change?"},
 {"id":"exponential-brain","cat":"philosophy","pic":"share","title":"Your brain has more connections than there are stars in the galaxy","story":"Your brain holds roughly 86 billion nerve cells, and each one may connect to thousands of others, making trillions of links. The number of possible paths through this network is larger than the count of stars in our galaxy. And it is not fixed: every time you learn something - a key, a word, a proof - the connections (সংযোগ - the links between brain cells) physically change. Learning is not filling a container; it is rewiring a living network. The brain you finish today's lesson with is not quite the one you started it with.","also":["The brain uses about a fifth of your body's energy though it weighs only a fiftieth.","Connections you use grow stronger; ones you ignore fade - 'use it or lose it'.","No two brains, not even twins', are wired the same way."],
  "think":"If learning physically rewires your brain, what does it mean to say you 'are' a fixed person?"},
 {"id":"friction-fire","cat":"physics","pic":"flame","title":"Rubbing turns motion into heat - and once made every fire","story":"When two surfaces slide, tiny bumps catch and drag; that resistance is friction (ঘর্ষণ - the force that resists sliding), and it turns the energy of motion into heat. Rub your hands and they warm; for most of human history, rubbing wood fast enough was how people made fire at all. Friction is why a ball rolling on grass stops, why brakes work, and why a spacecraft returning to Earth glows red-hot as it pushes through the air. The same humble force that slows you down also warms you, stops you, and once lit the first fires.","also":["A spacecraft re-entering the atmosphere can heat to over 1,500°C from friction.","Without friction you could not walk, write, or hold anything.","Oil works by slipping between surfaces so they barely touch."],
  "think":"If friction both helps and hinders, when would you want more of it and when less?"},
 {"id":"law-of-large-numbers","cat":"philosophy","pic":"scales","title":"Chance is wild up close and calm from far away","story":"Flip a coin ten times and you might get seven heads - chance looks lumpy. Flip it ten thousand times and heads will sit very near half. Single events are unpredictable, but huge numbers of them settle into steady patterns. This is the law of large numbers (বৃহৎ সংখ্যার সূত্র), and it is why insurance works, why casinos always win in the end, and why a poll of a few thousand people can predict millions. Randomness does not disappear; it averages out, and the average is astonishingly reliable.","also":["An insurance company cannot predict one house fire, but can predict thousands closely.","This is why a small, fair sample can speak for a huge population.","The next coin flip is still fifty-fifty, no matter what came before."],
  "think":"If single events stay unpredictable, how can the average of many be so sure?"},
 {"id":"water-anomaly","cat":"chemistry","pic":"ice","title":"Ice floats, and that is why there is life in frozen lakes","story":"Almost everything shrinks and grows denser as it cools. Water does too - until just before freezing, when it does the opposite and expands. So ice is lighter than the water below and floats. This small oddity is enormous: a lake freezes from the top down, leaving liquid water, and living things, safe beneath the ice. If ice sank, lakes would freeze solid from the bottom and little could survive the winter. A strange quirk in one common molecule (অণু - the smallest unit of a substance) helps make the planet livable.","also":["Water is densest at about 4°C, not at freezing.","The floating ice cap also reflects sunlight and helps cool the planet.","This same expansion is why a forgotten water bottle bursts in a freezer."],
  "think":"How many other 'small quirks' of nature might quietly be holding life together?"},
 {"id":"migration","cat":"biology","pic":"finch","title":"A small bird can cross an ocean without a map","story":"Some birds fly thousands of kilometres between summer and winter homes, a few without landing for days, crossing open ocean to an exact spot they have never seen. They navigate (পথ নির্ণয় - finding the way) by the Sun, the stars, landmarks, and even by sensing the Earth's magnetic field like a built-in compass. A young bird making its first journey alone still finds the way. No one teaches it the route; the knowledge is written into it, refined over countless generations by who arrived and who did not.","also":["The Arctic tern migrates pole to pole, seeing two summers a year.","Some birds can sleep one half of the brain at a time while flying.","Scientists think birds may actually 'see' the magnetic field as a pattern."],
  "think":"If a bird is born already knowing the route, what is the difference between instinct and learning?"},
 {"id":"entropy","cat":"physics","pic":"sand","title":"Why your room gets messy by itself but never tidy by itself","story":"There are countless ways for a room to be messy and only a few ways for it to be tidy, so random bumps almost always move things toward mess. This tendency toward disorder has a name - entropy (এনট্রপি - a measure of disorder) - and it is one of the deepest laws of physics. It is why heat flows from hot to cold, why a smell spreads across a room, and why time seems to run one way: the past is the tidier end. Order can be made, but only by spending energy - which is exactly what you do when you clean, or learn.","also":["This law is why no machine can ever be perfectly efficient.","A living body is a pocket of order, paid for by energy from food.","The 'arrow of time' - why we remember the past, not the future - may come from entropy."],
  "think":"If disorder always tends to grow, how does life manage to build such exquisite order?"},
 {"id":"redundancy-language","cat":"society","pic":"tablet","title":"You can rd ths sntnce bcs language wastes letters on purpose","story":"Written language is full of spare, predictable letters - and that is a feature, not a flaw. You can read a sentence with the vowels removed, or a word with a missing letter, because the extra is redundancy (বাড়তি তথ্য - information you could have guessed) that lets meaning survive a smudge, a typo, or a noisy phone line. Every communication system humans build - speech, writing, even the codes that carry your data - adds spare information on purpose, so that a message damaged on the way can still be understood.","also":["English text is roughly half predictable; you could guess much of it letter by letter.","Your phone uses error-correcting codes to fix data scrambled on the way.","This same redundancy is what lets autocomplete guess your next word."],
  "think":"If a message can survive being damaged, how much of what you 'read' are you actually guessing?"},
]

w = json.load(open("../wonders.json"))
have = {x["id"] for x in w["wonders"]}
for n in NEW:
    assert n["id"] not in have, n["id"]
    assert len(n["also"]) == 3
    assert all(k in n for k in ("id","cat","pic","title","story","also","think"))
w["wonders"].extend(NEW)
json.dump(w, open("../wonders.json", "w"), ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)
print("wonders now:", len(w["wonders"]))
